1
00:00:00,024 --> 00:00:04,980
Many people worry that a global satellite
network is constantly tracking the live

2
00:00:04,980 --> 00:00:07,140
location of their mobile phone.

3
00:00:07,810 --> 00:00:11,940
You might wonder how your phone determines
your exact position on Earth using

4
00:00:11,940 --> 00:00:15,580
signals from space without
ever sending any data back.

5
00:00:16,268 --> 00:00:21,600
It is a satellite-based navigation system
that provides geolocation and time

6
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:24,620
information to a receiver
anywhere near the Earth.

7
00:00:25,273 --> 00:00:29,240
The entire network does not require
you to transmit any data to function.

8
00:00:30,097 --> 00:00:34,880
Your device operates completely
independently of any telephone or internet

9
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:35,680
reception.

10
00:00:37,224 --> 00:00:39,940
To understand how this
passive network works,

11
00:00:40,220 --> 00:00:42,540
you need to look at the
space segment of the system.

12
00:00:43,268 --> 00:00:48,740
The space segment is composed of 24 to
32 space vehicles orbiting in medium

13
00:00:48,740 --> 00:00:49,540
Earth orbit.

14
00:00:50,109 --> 00:00:54,190
These satellites orbit at an
altitude of approximately 20,200

15
00:00:54,190 --> 00:00:55,716
kilometres.

16
00:00:56,155 --> 00:00:59,400
Each vehicle makes two complete
orbits each sidereal day,

17
00:01:00,020 --> 00:01:02,460
which is about 11 hours and 58 minutes.

18
00:01:03,242 --> 00:01:08,500
The satellites broadcast these vital data
points continuously down to the surface of

19
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:09,300
the planet.

20
00:01:10,621 --> 00:01:14,640
How do those radio waves turn into a
measurement of distance on your device?

21
00:01:15,474 --> 00:01:20,220
The time delay between when a satellite
transmits a signal and when a receiver gets

22
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:21,940
it is proportional to the distance.

23
00:01:22,720 --> 00:01:28,180
Modern electronics can measure this signal
offset to within about 10 nanoseconds for

24
00:01:28,180 --> 00:01:29,020
the civilian code.

25
00:01:30,771 --> 00:01:33,480
Once your device calculates
the distance to a satellite,

26
00:01:34,180 --> 00:01:37,000
it uses that data to narrow
down where you could be.

27
00:01:37,647 --> 00:01:40,360
If you know you are a certain
distance from a single satellite,

28
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:43,440
you could be anywhere on
a sphere surrounding it.

29
00:01:43,986 --> 00:01:49,180
Adding a second satellite narrows your
possible location down to the circle where

30
00:01:49,180 --> 00:01:50,340
two spheres intersect.

31
00:01:51,182 --> 00:01:55,940
Bringing in a third satellite shrinks the
intersection down to just two points in

32
00:01:55,940 --> 00:01:57,040
three-dimensional space.

33
00:01:57,834 --> 00:02:00,320
One of those two points
is usually out in space,

34
00:02:00,940 --> 00:02:04,120
leaving only a single realistic
position on the Earth.

35
00:02:05,810 --> 00:02:10,860
There is a major catch in this simple geometry
that requires an extra step from your

36
00:02:10,860 --> 00:02:11,660
receiver.

37
00:02:11,966 --> 00:02:16,740
That cheap clock has a time offset relative
to the true time kept by the satellite

38
00:02:16,740 --> 00:02:17,540
network.

39
00:02:18,024 --> 00:02:22,620
Because of this offset, your device
cannot measure true ranges and instead

40
00:02:22,620 --> 00:02:24,560
calculates pseudo-ranges.

41
00:02:25,013 --> 00:02:27,220
To solve for this extra unknown variable,

42
00:02:27,860 --> 00:02:30,980
your receiver must look at more
than just three space vehicles.

43
00:02:32,753 --> 00:02:35,120
Even with four satellites
and good geometry,

44
00:02:35,860 --> 00:02:40,660
tiny invisible physics forces threaten
to ruin the accuracy of your navigation.

45
00:02:41,652 --> 00:02:46,520
General relativity also shows that
clocks located deeper in a gravitational

46
00:02:46,520 --> 00:02:47,820
well tick slower.

47
00:02:48,550 --> 00:02:50,700
Because the satellites
are far above the Earth,

48
00:02:51,300 --> 00:02:56,760
their clocks run faster by
about 45,850 nanoseconds per day

49
00:02:57,070 --> 00:02:57,880
due to gravity.

50
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:02,880
Combined, these relativistic effects
cause satellite clocks to gain about

51
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:06,440
38.6 microseconds every single day.

52
00:03:07,116 --> 00:03:12,240
Without corrections, errors of roughly
11.4 km per day would accumulate

53
00:03:12,240 --> 00:03:13,160
in the position.

54
00:03:14,040 --> 00:03:19,220
Engineers solve this by giving the atomic
clocks on board a rate offset prior to

55
00:03:19,220 --> 00:03:21,340
launch to keep them perfectly synced.

56
00:03:22,877 --> 00:03:26,260
So how does GPS know where you
are without ever tracking you?

57
00:03:26,874 --> 00:03:32,060
Your phone simply listens to passive
radio signals broadcast by an overhead

58
00:03:32,060 --> 00:03:35,600
constellation of ultra-precise
floating atomic clocks.

59
00:03:36,412 --> 00:03:39,900
By measuring the time delay of
signals from four separate satellites,

60
00:03:40,540 --> 00:03:43,740
your device calculates its
exact position locally.

61
00:03:44,700 --> 00:03:46,340
No data travels back to space,

62
00:03:46,860 --> 00:03:51,800
leaving your location entirely private while
guiding you precisely across the world.
